{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgfby2tNTdCUs4uBF9qfH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For this tetrahedral Co(II) complex, **Dq = 407 cm⁻¹** and **B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**Assigning the bands**\n\nA tetrahedral d⁷ ion has the same quartet term pattern as an octahedral d³ ion. The ground state is ⁴A₂(F), and the three spin-allowed transitions are:\n\n- ν₁ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂(F) = 4 070 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F) = 7 030 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P) = 16 400 cm⁻¹\n\n**Finding Dq**\n\nThe first band equals the ligand-field splitting directly:\n\nν₁ = 10Dq, so Dq = 4 070 / 10 = **407 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**Finding B**\n\nThe two ⁴T₁ levels mix with each other. In the secular determinant for these levels, the sum of the two energies is:\n\nν₂ + ν₃ = 15B + 30Dq\n\nPutting in the numbers:\n\n- 7 030 + 16 400 = 23 430 cm⁻¹\n- 30Dq = 12 210 cm⁻¹\n- 15B = 23 430 − 12 210 = 11 220 cm⁻¹\n- B ≈ **748 cm⁻¹**\n\n**Checking the result**\n\nThe product of the roots must also fit the data. The required condition is:\n\n340Dq² − 18Dq(ν₂ + ν₃) + ν₂ν₃ = 0\n\nWith these values the left side comes to about −3.6 × 10⁴. That is only about 0.03% of the individual terms, which are around 10⁸. So the three bands fit the d⁷ tetrahedral model consistently.\n\n**Interpretation**\n\nThe free Co²⁺ ion has B ≈ 1 120 cm⁻¹, so the ratio β ≈ 0.67. This shows a substantial nephelauxetic (covalency) effect, which is typical for tetrahedral Co(II).\n\nThe Racah parameter C cannot be found from these bands. All three are quartet → quartet transitions, and their energies depend only on Dq and B.\n\n{\"Dq\": 407, \"B\": 748, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq from nu1=10Dq and B from nu2+nu3=15B+30Dq; C is not determined by quartet bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1648,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m